{
  "module": "Output side: from scores to text",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "generation-loop",
    "title": "Generating text one token at a time",
    "summary": "How a reply is produced: the loop of running the model, sampling a token, appending it and running again, what autoregressive means, and why a long reply needs many passes.",
    "covers": ["autoregressive-generation"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "next-token-sampling",
        "reason": "Each step of the loop samples one token from the next-token distribution."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "sequence-shapes",
        "reason": "The loop grows the sequence length n by one each step, so n must already be clear."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "one-token",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "One pass gives one distribution, and sampling turns that into "
            },
            {
              "text": "one token",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". So a single pass is enough to add a single token to a text, not to write a reply."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "To write a whole reply, the model does the same thing again and again."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 20)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "the-loop",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "This is the generation loop:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Run the model on the tokens so far"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Turn the logits at the last position into probabilities with softmax"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Sample one token from those probabilities"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Append that token to the end of the text, then go back to the first step"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Only the last position's distribution is needed, because that is the one that predicts what comes next."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 18, 20)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "walk-through",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy walk-through, with invented choices. To keep it simple, assume each word is one token, so the text starts as "
            },
            {
              "text": "The cat",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", which is 2 tokens:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Pass 1 on "
              },
              {
                "text": "The cat",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": " (n = 2). Sample "
              },
              {
                "text": "sat",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": ". The text is now "
              },
              {
                "text": "The cat sat",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": " (n = 3)."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Pass 2 on "
              },
              {
                "text": "The cat sat",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": " (n = 3). Sample "
              },
              {
                "text": "on",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": ". The text is now "
              },
              {
                "text": "The cat sat on",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": " (n = 4)."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Pass 3 on "
              },
              {
                "text": "The cat sat on",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": " (n = 4). Sample "
              },
              {
                "text": "the",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": ". The text is now "
              },
              {
                "text": "The cat sat on the",
                "italic": true
              },
              {
                "text": " (n = 5)."
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Each pass starts from a text one token longer than the one before, because the chosen token is added to the input."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 16, 20)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "autoregressive",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Generating a sequence one element at a time, with each element predicted from all the earlier ones, is called "
            },
            {
              "text": "autoregressive",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " generation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The earlier elements include the model's "
            },
            {
              "text": "own earlier outputs",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". A token it chose two steps ago is now just part of the input, and every later choice is made in light of it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 16)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "cost",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "This is why a reply of 200 tokens takes about "
            },
            {
              "text": "200 forward passes",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": each pass yields a distribution for one next token, that token is appended, and the model runs again."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The prompt is different. The tokens you supply are all known in advance, so they are processed together in one parallel pass. Every token the model generates then needs its own pass."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A trick called the KV cache, covered later in the course, makes each of those later passes much cheaper."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 20)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Why does generating a 200-token reply take about 200 forward passes?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Each pass gives a distribution for one next token, which is appended before the model runs again"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The reply is built one token per pass."
